ounded linear map\nL741@P9: of the nuclear space H(D) to any Banach space is not only nuclear but nuclear\nL742@P9: of order zero [7]. For more details about the nuclear spaces we refer to [7, 5, 16].\nL743@P9: Now, to prove the nuclearity of the transfer operator, we extend it first to\nL744@P9: the nuclear space H(Dr) ⊃ B(Dr) with r ∈ (1,\nL745@P9: 1+√\nL746@P9: 5\nL747@P9: 2\nL748@P9: ), that is\nL749@P9: Lbs : H(Dr) → B(Dr)\nL750@P9: Lbsf(z) = P∞\nL751@P9: n=1(\nL752@P9: 1\nL753@P9: z + n\nL754@P9: )\nL755@P9: 2sf(\nL756@P9: 1\nL757@P9: z + n\nL758@P9: ), (57)\nL759@P9: Lemma 5. The operator Lbs : H(Dr) → B(Dr) for Re(s) > 1\nL760@P9: 2\nL761@P9: is nuclear of\nL762@P9: order zero where r ∈ (1,\nL763@P9: 1+√\nL764@P9: 5\nL765@P9: 2\nL766@P9: ).\nL767@P9: Proof. As mentioned before, every bounded linear map of H(Dr) to a Banach\nL768@P9: space is nuclear of order zero. Thus it is enough to prove the boundedness\nL769@P9: of Lbs. To this end we should show that there exists a neighbourhood of zero\nL770@P9: V (0) ⊂ H(Dr) which is mapped to a bounded subset of B(Dr). To begin with\nL771@P9: we define the following sequence of open discs,\nL772@P9: Kn = {w = ψn(z) | z ∈ Dr} (58)\nL773@P9: whose radius rn and centre cn is given by\nL774@P9: rn =\nL775@P9: r\nL776@P9: (n + 1)2 − r\nL777@P9: 2\nL778@P9: (59)\nL779@P9: and\nL780@P9: cn = ( n + 1\nL781@P9: (n + 1)2 − r\nL782@P9: 2\nL783@P9: , 0). (60)\nL784@P9: We choose the following neighbourhood of zero V (0) ⊂ H(Dr),\nL785@P9: V (0) = (f ∈ H(Dr) | sup\nL786@P9: z∈K1\nL787@P9: |f(z)| < M)(61)\nL788@P9: where M > 0 is a constant. Also the following holds\nL789@P9: Kn ⊂ K1, n ∈ N. (62)\nL790@P9-10: 10For any f ∈ H(Dr) we have\nL791@P10: sup\nL792@P10: z∈Dr\nL793@P10: |Lbsf(z)| ≤ X∞\nL794@P10: n=1\nL795@P10: (\nL796@P10: 1\nL797@P10: 1 − r + n\nL798@P10: )\nL799@P10: 2σ\nL800@P10: sup\nL801@P10: z∈Dr\nL802@P10: f(ψn(z)). (63)\nL803@P10: But because of (62),\nL804@P10: sup\nL805@P10: z∈Dr\nL806@P10: f(ψn(z)) ≤ sup\nL807@P10: z∈K1\nL808@P10: f(z) = M. (64)\nL809@P10: Inserting this into (63) completes the proof.\nL810@P10: On the other hand we have\nL811@P10: Ls = Lbs ◦ ı\nL812@P10: where ı is the bounded injection given by\nL813@P10: ı : B(Dr) → H(Dr), ı(f) = f.\nL814@P10: Then nuclearity of Lbs leads to nuclearity of Ls because of the fact that the\nL815@P10: product of a bounded operator with a nuclear operator is also nuclear with the\nL816@P10: same order. Thus we have the following corrolary,\nL817@P10: Corollary 2. The Mayer transfer operator for the groups P GL(2, Z) and P SL(2, Z)\nL818@P10: in the domain Re(s) >\nL819@P10: 1\nL820@P10: 2\nL821@P10: is nuclear of order zero.\nL822@P10: Note that in this corrolary we concluded the nuclearity of the transfer oper\u0002ator for P SL(2, Z) from the nuclearity of the transfer operator for P GL(2, Z)\nL823@P10: and (15).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://arxiv.org/pdf/1008.4229)\nciteturn124view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 29; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn122view2\",\"pattern\":\"det(1\"}); Total lines: 2561\nL551@P7: For certain classes of nuclear operators a trace functional is available. Ac\u0002cording to a remarkable result of Grothendieck, nuclear operators L of order\nL552@P7: q ≤ 2\nL553@P7: 3\nL554@P7: have a trace tr=\nL555@P7: Pρi as the sum of eigenvalues ρi counted with multi\u0002plicities. Moreover the Fredholm determinant of L is defined as\nL556@P7: det(1 − zL) = exp tr log(1 − zL) = Y\nL557@P7: i\nL558@P7: (1 − ρiz) (42)\nL559@P7: or equivalently\nL560@P7: det(1 − zL) = exp(−\nL561@P7: X∞\nL562@P7: n=1\nL563@P7: z\nL564@P7: n\nL565@P7: n\nL566@P7: trL\nL567@P7: n\nL568@P7: ). (43)\nL569@P7: Consequentely if L = L(s) is a holomorphic function of a parameter s in a\nL1638@P17: In the following lemma we see the close connection of the Mayer transfer oper\u0002ator and the dynamical function ξ(s).\nL163